## 4. main-page.css — Deep Dive

This file styles the **home/index page** with a dense hero grid, a bento-layout card grid, and sticky-scroll stack cards.

---

### 4.1 Dense Hero Grid (`.hero__grid--dense`)

```css
.hero__grid--dense {
    background-size: 60px 60px;
    opacity: 0.04;
    background-image:
        linear-gradient(var(--ink) 1px, transparent 1px),
        linear-gradient(90deg, var(--ink) 1px, transparent 1px);
}
```

A very faint grid overlay on the hero background — pure CSS, no images needed. At `opacity: 0.04` it's barely visible, just adding industrial texture.

---

### 4.2 Bento Grid (`.bento__grid`)

A 12-column grid with auto-rows — the "bento box" layout popular in modern design.

```css
.bento__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr);
    grid-auto-rows: minmax(180px, auto);
    gap: var(--gap);
}
```

**Column span classes:**

| Class | Columns |
|---|---|
| `.bento-card--wide` | 8 of 12 |
| `.bento-card--mid` | 6 of 12 (half) |
| `.bento-card--narrow` | 4 of 12 (third) |
| `.bento-card--tall` | `grid-row: span 2` (double height) |

**Breakpoints:**
- `≤ 1024px` → Wide, mid, and narrow all collapse to `span 6`
- `≤ 768px` → All collapse to `1 / -1` (full width), tall loses double height

**Decorative circle on cards:**
```css
.bento-card__visual {
    position: absolute;
    right: -10px;
    bottom: -10px;
    width: 120px;
    height: 120px;
    border: 3px solid currentColor;
    border-radius: 50%;
    opacity: 0.08;
}
```

A decorative circle bleeds slightly outside the card — a subtle industrial/technical aesthetic touch.

---

### 4.3 Sticky Stack Cards (`.stack__card`)

This is the most complex component in the main page — cards that **stack on top of each other as you scroll** using CSS `position: sticky`.

```css
.stack__card {
    position: sticky;
    top: 80px;   /* First card sticks at 80px from top */
    border: var(--border);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow);
    background: var(--base);
    padding: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 4rem);
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr);
    gap: var(--gap);
    transition: none;
}
```

**Each subsequent card has a higher `top` value** so they stack with an offset:

```css
.stack__card:nth-child(1) { top: 80px;  background: var(--base); }
.stack__card:nth-child(2) { top: 110px; background: #F5F5F0; }
.stack__card:nth-child(3) { top: 140px; background: var(--ink); color: var(--base); }
.stack__card:nth-child(4) { top: 170px; background: #F0F0EB; }
```

As you scroll, card 1 sticks, then card 2 slides over it and sticks slightly lower, then card 3 covers both, etc. This creates a layered "stacking deck" scroll experience.

**Card 3 (dark) is special** — it has the `--ink` background and orange accent shadow:
```css
.stack__card:nth-child(3) { box-shadow: var(--shadow-safety); }
.stack__card:nth-child(3):hover { box-shadow: 12px 12px 0px var(--safety); }
```

**Internal card layout:**

- Left (`.stack__card-left`) → spans 4 columns → large step number
- Right (`.stack__card-right`) → spans 8 columns → title, description, tags

The step number:
```css
.stack__card-step {
    font-size: clamp(4rem, 8vw, 7rem);
    opacity: 0.12;   /* Very faint — purely decorative */
    letter-spacing: -0.05em;
}
```

**On mobile** (`≤ 768px`): `position: relative` overrides sticky — cards just stack normally in the flow. The `top: auto !important` removes the sticky offset.

